# Changes in version 0.1.10 (in development)

o FIX: `transformer_scores()` failed with "ImportError: ... requires the protobuf library but it was not found in your environment" after the model weights loaded. This happened for zero-shot models whose tokenizer is a SentencePiece model without a `tokenizer.json`, such as the multilingual `joeddav/xlm-roberta-large-xnli`. transformers needs the Python package protobuf to convert these tokenizers. Versions up to 0.1.7 got protobuf indirectly through qdrant-client and TensorFlow, which 0.1.8 removed. protobuf is now a core requirement and is in `python_requirements()`. The first online session after the update builds a new Python environment. Offline setups (`setup_cache(offline = TRUE)`) must repeat the preparation once with network access: `setup_cache()` with the same path and `gpu` value but without `offline = TRUE`, then the same `setup_modules()` call. If Python already runs when transforEmotion loads, that Python must also have protobuf. A fixed environment (`TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON`) or an image built from `docker/Dockerfile` does not change: rebuild it from `python_requirements()`, or install protobuf into it. For a read-only image, see `?python_requirements`

o CHANGE: when a model's tokenizer needs protobuf and the Python that reticulate uses does not have it, `transformer_scores()` names that Python and says how to install protobuf into it

o ADD: the help page of `transformer_scores()` names a multilingual model for text that is not in English (`joeddav/xlm-roberta-large-xnli`; there is no `facebook/xlm-roberta-large-xnli`) and shows a Spanish example. The README shows the same example, and the setup vignette and the Colab notebook name the model

o CHANGE: `rag()` and `rag_sentemo()` no longer offer `transformer = "Qwen3-1.7B"`. This choice never loaded a model. It named `Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B-Instruct`, which does not exist on the Hugging Face Hub (the Qwen3 1.7B and 0.6B models are `Qwen/Qwen3-1.7B` and `Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B`, without an Instruct variant). Qwen3 also needs transformers >= 4.51, which the Python environment that transforEmotion installs does not have. The help pages also named a `"Qwen3-0.6B"` choice that neither function accepted. A `transformer` value that starts with `"Qwen3"` now stops with this reason before Python starts. Use `"Qwen2.5-1.5B"` or `"Qwen2.5-3B"` instead

o FIX: `rag()` with `transformer = "Gemma3-1B"` or `"Gemma3-4B"` now checks the installed transformers version before it frees the previous model or downloads Gemma 3. When the version is older than 4.50, it stops and explains why. Gemma 3 needs transformers >= 4.50. The Python environment that transforEmotion installs caps transformers below 4.47 for its llama-index 0.10 stack, so Gemma 3 works only with a fixed environment (`TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON`) that has transformers >= 4.50. The help pages of `rag()` and `rag_sentemo()` now state this requirement, and the `rag()` help page lists the huggingface-hub and llama-index versions that such an environment also needs. The `validate_rag_predictions()` example now uses `"Qwen2.5-3B"`, which loads in the installed environment

o FIX: the help page of `rag()` described `ministral/Ministral-3b-instruct` as Mistral's compact 3B Instruct model. It is a community repository with the Mistral architecture, and Mistral AI did not publish it

o FIX: the help page of `sentence_similarity()` had the title and description of `transformer_scores()`. It now says that the function returns the cosine similarity of each pair of `text` and `comparison_text`. The example model for `transformer` was a zero-shot classification model (`typeform/distilbert-base-uncased-mnli`). It is now `sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2`. The error for a model without a working pipeline linked to the zero-shot classification models on Hugging Face. It now links to the sentence similarity models

o FIX: the error of `sentence_similarity()` for a missing `comparison_text` printed a bell character in its example

o FIX: when `sentence_similarity()` loaded a model again without `device` after a `device_map` error and that second load also failed, it stopped with "object 'classifier' not found". It now stops with the error of the second load

o FIX: `sentence_similarity()` with `keep_in_env = TRUE` and a classifier reused from `envir` stopped with "object 'sentence_transformers' not found" unless the global environment had a copy of the module. This happened, for example, on the second call with `envir` set to an environment

o CHANGE: `sentence_similarity()` moves a classifier reused from `envir` to the device that `device = "cpu"` or `device = "cuda"` sets. Before, it used the classifier on the device it was loaded on. With `device = "auto"` the classifier stays where it is

o FIX: `evaluate_emotions()` pairs `probs_cols[i]` with `classes[i]` for AUROC. Without `classes`, it used the sorted class labels, so probability columns in another order were scored against the wrong class and the per-class and macro AUROC were wrong without a warning. With the vignette data, columns in the order anger, joy, sadness, fear, surprise gave AUROC 0.318 for fear, 0.381 for joy and 0.404 for sadness instead of 1. The classes now take the order of `probs_cols`, matched by column name without a `prob_` prefix if every column has one. The confusion matrix and the per-class tables follow the same order. Results do not change when `probs_cols` are in sorted order. If the column names do not match the class labels, the classes stay sorted and `evaluate_emotions()` gives a warning

o FIX: when `evaluate_emotions()` made predictions from the highest probability in `probs_cols` (no prediction column, or missing predictions) and `classes` was not given, the predictions were the column names, such as `prob_anger`, and never matched the truth. They are now the class names

o CHANGE: with `probs_cols` and without `classes`, `evaluate_emotions()` leaves out a class of `probs_cols` that is in neither the truth nor the prediction column and computes AUROC for the other classes. Before, AUROC was `NA` with the warning "Number of probability columns does not match number of classes". The macro F1, precision and recall do not change

o FIX: the "Complete Pipeline Example" in the `evaluate_emotions()` vignette called `transformer_scores()` with arguments that it does not have (`x`, `return_prob`) and read `predicted_class` and `prob_<class>` columns that its result does not contain. The example now passes `text`, binds the class probabilities into one row per text and takes the class with the highest probability as the prediction. Three other calls in the vignette now pass `classes` in the order of `probs_cols`, and the install chunk names CRAN and the GitHub repository. A new test checks that every R chunk of every vignette parses and that every call to an exported function uses arguments that the function has

o FIX: the setup vignette showed printed output in R chunks that do not parse as R, and `purl` copied them into the R code of the vignette as comments. They are now plain text blocks. The vignette no longer says that the package implements only zero-shot classification models: the scoring models are zero-shot, and `sentence_similarity()` uses sentence embedding models

o FIX: `vad_scores(..., transformer = "...")` stopped with "formal argument "transformer" matched by multiple actual arguments". `vad_scores(..., classes = ...)`, or an abbreviation such as `class = ...`, stopped with "the condition has length > 1". `vad_scores()` now stops with a message that names the argument and says to give the model as `model`. A `model` that is not one character string (for example `NULL`) also stops with a clear message

o FIX: `vad_scores(label_type = "custom")` required `custom_labels` for all three dimensions, also when `dimensions` asked for fewer. For this reason the custom-label example on the help page (valence only) failed. Now `custom_labels` must give only the dimensions in `dimensions`

o FIX: `vad_scores()` also warned "Definitional labels failed ... Falling back to simple labels" and retried with the simple labels for `label_type = "simple"` and `"custom"`. The fallback now applies only to definitional labels, so the simple labels never replace custom labels

o FIX: `transformer_scores()` ignored `local_model_path` for the built-in names `"cross-encoder-roberta"`, `"cross-encoder-distilroberta"` and `"facebook-bart"`. It loaded the model from the Hugging Face cache or the network. It now loads the model from `local_model_path`, as the help page says. This also fixes `vad_scores(..., local_model_path = ...)` with `model = "auto"`. A classifier kept with `keep_in_env = TRUE` is now reused only if both calls give the same `local_model_path`, or neither gives one. Before, a kept classifier answered every later call with the same `transformer` name

o CHANGE: the help page of `vad_scores()` says what `model = "auto"` selects: `"cross-encoder-distilroberta"` for text and `"oai-base"` for images and video. It says that any other value goes unchanged to `transformer_scores()`, `image_scores()` or `video_scores()`, and it names the multilingual text model `joeddav/xlm-roberta-large-xnli`. The built-in VAD labels are in English. Use `label_type = "custom"` for labels in another language. Details now quotes the definitional labels that the function sends, and its bold headings no longer show as literal asterisks

o FIX: offline sessions (`UV_OFFLINE`, for example from `setup_cache(offline = TRUE)`) whose uv cache lacks a declared Python package, for example after an update of transforEmotion adds a requirement, failed at the first analysis with "Installation of Python not found, Python bindings not loaded". The first function that uses Python now checks the cache with uv before Python starts. If a package is missing, it stops with uv's reason and the lines to run once with network access: `setup_cache()` with the same path and `gpu` value, then `setup_modules()` with the extras the session uses. Without `setup_cache()`, the lines unset `UV_OFFLINE` and keep the session's uv cache and PyTorch build. After the cache is filled, the same session can run the analysis again. The check is skipped when reticulate will use another Python, for example from `VIRTUAL_ENV` or a `.venv` folder. When a feature set cannot be added to a running Python offline, the error also gives these lines

o ADD: with `TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON` set, `setup_modules()` compares the fixed environment with the requirements of the core set and of `extras`. It gives a warning that names each package that is missing or has a version outside its requirement, with a `uv pip install` command for that environment and the `python_requirements()` call to rebuild it, with `gpu = TRUE` for a CUDA build of PyTorch

o CHANGE: with `TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON` set, a function that needs an optional feature set (`rag`, `youtube`, `audio`, `findingemo` or `gpu`) first checks that the fixed environment has the Python modules of that set. If modules are missing, the function stops with an error that names them and says that transforEmotion does not install packages into a fixed environment. The error gives a `uv pip install` command for the packages of the missing sets, the `python_requirements()` call to rebuild the environment, and the `EXTRAS` build argument of `docker/Dockerfile`, for example `--build-arg EXTRAS=rag`. The rebuild call and the build argument also name the sets that the session found in the environment. Before, for example, `rag()` in an image without the rag set stopped with "ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'llama_index'". The check finds the modules with `importlib.util.find_spec()` and does not import them. A feature set that passes is not checked again in the session. For EVA-CLIP, `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` need the gpu set only when the fixed environment has the CUDA build of PyTorch. With the CPU build, for example in the CPU Docker image on a host with a GPU, EVA-CLIP loads without 4-bit quantization, as before

o CHANGE: `load_findingemo_annotations()` no longer declares the findingemo Python packages (findingemo-light, termcolor). Only `download_findingemo_data()` uses them. A dataset in the flat structure (`annotations.csv` and `urls.json`) is read without Python, and other datasets need only the core packages. For this reason an offline session or a fixed environment (`TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON`) without the findingemo set can read a dataset that was downloaded on another machine

o CHANGE: with `TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON` set, for example in the Docker image, `library(transforEmotion)` no longer suggests `te_cleanup_default_venv()` when `~/.virtualenvs/r-reticulate` exists. A fixed environment does not use that folder

o CHANGE: `setup_cache()` also sets `HF_HUB_CACHE` and `SENTENCE_TRANSFORMERS_HOME` to the model folder of the cache, `<path>/huggingface/hub`. These variables take precedence over `HF_HOME`. Before, a value of either variable, for example from the Docker image, kept the models out of the cache folder. It also unsets `TRANSFORMERS_CACHE`, `PYTORCH_TRANSFORMERS_CACHE` and `PYTORCH_PRETRAINED_BERT_CACHE`, which transformers reads before `HF_HUB_CACHE`

o CHANGE: `docker/Dockerfile` checks the image at build time. The build fails if a Python module of the requested feature sets does not import, if a `GPU=1` build has the CPU build of PyTorch, or if the default text, image and sentence models do not load without network

o CHANGE: an image built from `docker/Dockerfile` lists what it contains in `/opt/transforEmotion`: `BUILD_INFO` (transforEmotion commit, build date, versions of R, Python, PyTorch and uv, build arguments), `python-packages.txt`, `requirements.lock`, `R-packages.txt` and `models.txt`. Its labels name transforEmotion and the commit; before, it had the labels of its base image, rocker/r-ver. The base images are pinned by digest (rocker/r-ver 4.6.1, uv 0.13.6)

o CHANGE: the image compiles the Python bytecode at build time, so each R session in a read-only image (Apptainer) starts about 5 seconds faster. The Python layer is about 220 MB larger, but the image is about 120 MB smaller to download, because the R layer no longer keeps package caches and the source tree

o CHANGE: the image sets `HF_HUB_CACHE`, `SENTENCE_TRANSFORMERS_HOME`, `RETICULATE_PYTHON` and `RETICULATE_PYTHON_ENV`. These variables from the host (Apptainer passes host variables into the container) no longer hide the models or the Python environment of the image. A host `TRANSFORMERS_CACHE` or `PYTORCH_TRANSFORMERS_CACHE` still hides the models from transformers: unset it before `apptainer exec`, or use `apptainer exec --cleanenv`. To use a model folder of your own in the image, set `HF_HUB_CACHE` and `SENTENCE_TRANSFORMERS_HOME` to it, not `HF_HOME`, or call `setup_cache()`

o FIX: in a read-only image, transformers printed two warnings in each session: "You are offline and the cache for model files in Transformers v4.22.0 has been updated", and that it could not write to the cache folder. The models loaded correctly. The image now has the cache version file that transformers reads

# Changes in version 0.1.9

o ADD: `rag()` and `rag_sentemo()` support Qwen 2.5 Instruct (`transformer = "Qwen2.5-1.5B"` or `"Qwen2.5-3B"`), including structured outputs (`output = "json"`, `"table"`, `"csv"`). The Qwen models are not gated, so no Hugging Face token is needed, and they load with the transformers version the package installs (Gemma 3 needs transformers >= 4.50)

o CHANGE: for the HuggingFace models, `rag()` now passes `prompt` to the model as the system prompt (it was ignored before); for Qwen 2.5 every query is formatted with the tokenizer's chat template (`is_chat_model = TRUE` in llama-index)

o FIX: `rag()` failed with "got multiple values for keyword argument 'max_new_tokens'" when `max_new_tokens` was set for a HuggingFace model

o FIX: when a Gemma 3 model failed to load for a reason other than authentication (for example an old transformers version), `rag()` swallowed the error and silently kept the previously loaded model

o FIX: `rag()` read retrieved nodes through a property that raises in llama-index >= 0.13; it now uses `get_content()`

o FIX: `rag()` frees the previously loaded LLM before it loads another one. Both stayed in memory, so switching from TinyLLAMA to Qwen2.5-3B on a 16 GB machine was refused or killed

o FIX: `rag(transformer = "TinyLLAMA", max_new_tokens = ...)` failed on every document with the same "got multiple values for keyword argument 'max_new_tokens'" error

o CHANGE: `rag(response_mode = "no_text")` only retrieves, so it no longer loads an LLM; a model loaded by an earlier call is kept

o FIX: the memory check before `rag()` loads a model on the CPU counts the 32-bit weights and the extra memory used while loading (Qwen2.5-3B needs about 14 GB, not 6 GB), reads the memory available now on Linux from `/proc/meminfo`, and respects a cgroup memory limit (containers, Colab, systemd), which `free` does not show. On a GPU it checks free rather than total memory

o FIX: `sentence_similarity()` failed for models given by name with the default `device = "auto"`, and ignored `device` for the default model

o FIX: `emoxicon_scores()` returned a data frame that tidyr and dplyr rejected (its class put "emoxicon" after "data.frame"; the `emotions` dataset had the same order), and it no longer installs the unused `emoxicon` package from GitHub

o FIX: the printed summary of `evaluate_emotions()` read the second and third columns as truth and prediction instead of `truth_col` and `pred_col`

o FIX: `validate_rag_json()` rejected `evidence_chunks` read with `jsonlite::fromJSON()`, which turns the array of chunks into a data frame

o CHANGE: no function uses `remotes` any more, since `emoxicon_scores()` no longer installs emoxicon. It stays in Suggests for the README and the first-run workflow, which install transforEmotion from GitHub

# Changes in version 0.1.8

o CHANGE: `remotes` moved from Imports to Suggests. Only `emoxicon_scores()` uses it, to install emoxicon from GitHub, and it is loaded then rather than when transforEmotion is attached

o FIX: `library(transforEmotion)` failed on machines without OpenGL (`libGLU.so.1`) and warned about a missing display during `R CMD check`, because importing `LSAfun` loaded `rgl` at startup. `LSAfun` is now suggested and is loaded only by `nlp_scores()`, which is the only function that uses it

o FIX: the Krippendorff reference on `annotation_agreement()` pointed at a University of Pennsylvania repository URL that now redirects, and the SNLI project page linked from `transformer_scores()` no longer accepts HTTPS connections. The help pages use the current repository item and the Hugging Face copy of the SNLI corpus

o FIX: `map_discrete_to_vad(cache_lexicon = TRUE)` (the default) failed after loading the NRC VAD lexicon, because it tried to store the lexicon in the locked package namespace. An empty list, as from `transformer_scores()` with no texts, failed with "arguments imply differing number of rows"

o ADD: `speech_scores()` scores segments of the audio track of an audio file, a video file or a YouTube video against user-defined classes with LAION CLAP (`laion/larger_clap_music_and_speech`, zero-shot), one row per segment with its start and end time. `nsegments`, `segment_length` (at most 10 s), `start`, `end` and `uniform` choose the segments, as `nframes`, `start`, `end` and `uniform` choose the frames in `video_scores()`. Audio is read with PyAV (`av`, which bundles FFmpeg), added on first use; prepare it with `setup_modules(extras = "audio")`. For YouTube URLs only the audio is downloaded

o FIX: `video_scores()` and `detect_faces()` failed for every YouTube URL with "Failed to get video stream after 3 attempts", because YouTube no longer offers progressive streams (video and audio in one file). They now download the highest H.264 video-only MP4 at 720p or below when no progressive stream exists, so a video kept with `save_video = TRUE` has no sound

o FIX: `video_scores()` and `detect_faces()` treated every input that contained "youtu" as a YouTube URL, so a local file such as `youtube/clip.mp4` was sent to the YouTube download and failed. Only http(s) URLs on youtube.com or youtu.be count as YouTube videos now, as in `speech_scores()`

o CHANGE: faster, smaller Python setup. The first environment build downloads about 500 MB instead of about 3.4 GB on Linux, and later sessions reuse the cached environment in under a second

o CHANGE: PyTorch is installed as a CPU-only build unless an NVIDIA GPU is detected (CUDA 12.6 build on Linux and Windows); override with `TE_FORCE_CPU=1` or `TRANSFOREMOTION_USE_GPU=1`

o CHANGE: Python 3.12 instead of 3.10; transformers 4.46, sentence-transformers 5.x and opencv-python-headless replace the older pins; TensorFlow, which was never used, is no longer installed

o CHANGE: packages for optional features are added on first use: LlamaIndex in `rag()`, `pytubefix` in `video_scores()` for YouTube URLs, `findingemo-light` in the FindingEmo functions and `bitsandbytes` for EVA-CLIP-8B on a GPU

o CHANGE: `rag()` installs `llama-index-core` instead of the `llama-index` meta-package, so `llama-index-legacy` and the OpenAI integrations are no longer installed

o CHANGE: `setup_modules()` gains `extras` and `download_models` arguments and now downloads the default models; `setup_gpu_modules()` now installs only `bitsandbytes` (AutoAWQ, Auto-GPTQ, Optimum and llama-cpp-python were never used)

o CHANGE: uv is no longer installed by the package or prompted for when it is attached; reticulate downloads uv itself when needed. Requires reticulate >= 1.45.0

o ADD: `setup_cache()` keeps the Python environment, Python itself and the models in one folder and can run offline, for HPC clusters whose compute nodes have no internet access

o ADD: `python_requirements()` returns the Python requirements, and `TRANSFOREMOTION_PYTHON` makes transforEmotion use a fixed Python environment, for read-only environments such as Apptainer images

o ADD: `docker/Dockerfile` builds an offline image with the package, a fixed Python environment and the default models (CPU or CUDA); a GitHub Actions workflow publishes it to GHCR

o ADD: `setup_modules()` gains a `models` argument for additional Hugging Face models, and with `extras = "rag"` also downloads the rag() models and the NLTK data LlamaIndex needs

o FIX: installation failed on Apple Silicon Macs because `tensorflow-cpu` has no macOS arm64 wheels, and the failure was hidden

o FIX: on NVIDIA GPUs, `rag()` returned empty results because Triton, which PyTorch uses for some GPU kernels, could not compile its helper ("Python.h: No such file or directory"); the package now points C compilers at the Python headers when Python starts

o FIX: `rag()` warned that structured outputs need Gemma on every call with the default `output`, because the check ran before `output` was resolved

o ADD: Google Colab notebook (`notebooks/transforEmotion_colab.ipynb`) with text, image, video and RAG demos, linked from the README

o FIX: `video_scores()` deleted every .jpg file in `save_dir` when `save_frames = FALSE`, and built wrong paths (so frames were left behind, with a warning per frame) when `save_dir` had no trailing slash; it now deletes only the frames it wrote

o FIX: the startup message printed raw ANSI escape codes in Jupyter and Google Colab notebooks; text is now styled only in RStudio and terminals

o FIX: `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` report a missing `local_model_path` directory instead of "Model not recognized"

o ADD: vision models "oai-base-fer" and "oai-large-fer", OpenAI CLIP B/32 and L/14 with the vision encoder fine-tuned on FER2013 (tanganke/clip-vit-*_fer2013). On MAFW video clips, accuracy for seven basic emotions rose from 0.33 to 0.48 with `oai-large-fer` (labels such as "a photo of an angry face"); see the README

o ADD: `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` accept Hugging Face checkpoints that hold only a fine-tuned CLIP vision encoder; the text encoder and processor come from the base CLIP model named in the checkpoint's config

o FIX: `model = "jina-v2"` failed with "'tokenizers.models.Unigram' object has no attribute 'end_of_word_suffix'": models registered as "clip-custom" (Jina CLIP v2, EVA-CLIP-8B) were loaded with the standard CLIP adapter. They now get their own adapters, and the Jina adapter uses the model's own multilingual tokenizer and image preprocessing (`encode_text()`, `encode_image()`) instead of the English OpenAI CLIP tokenizer, so labels in other languages work

o FIX: on Linux, PyTorch computed matrix products with R's BLAS instead of its own MKL, because R's BLAS is loaded first and both export `sgemm_`; with R's reference BLAS, CPU inference was tens of times slower (Jina CLIP v2: about 330 s instead of 11 s per image). torch is now imported with `RTLD_DEEPBIND` when Python starts

o FIX: `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` reloaded the vision model on every call, because re-sourcing the Python module emptied the model cache

o CHANGE: citation now points to the published article in Computational Communication Research (doi:10.5117/CCR2026.2.2.TOMA); see `citation("transforEmotion")`

o ADD: `detect_faces()` finds every face in images or in the frames sampled from a video and returns one row per face with its box, confidence, five landmarks, a track id that follows the face across video frames, and a `person_id` shared by faces of the same person (across images, or across all tracks of a video, so a person who leaves and comes back keeps one id). `select_faces()` keeps faces by rule, face number, track or person, and `face_scores()` scores exactly those faces

o ADD: `draw_faces()`, or `plot()` on a `detect_faces()` result, draws each face's box with its face or track number and person on the image or video frame, in one colour per person, and shows the drawn images in the plot window or below a notebook cell

o ADD: `view_faces()` shows the faces of a `detect_faces()` table in the web browser, coloured by person, with a list of the people found. R serves the page itself (with httpuv, on 127.0.0.1 only). With `select = TRUE`, faces, video tracks or people can be clicked, and Send to R returns the chosen faces from `view_faces()`. With `live = FALSE`, the page is a folder that also works when opened from disk, and `read_face_selection()` reads its exported choice

o ADD: in a Jupyter notebook, including Google Colab, `view_faces()` shows the page below the cell (`inline = TRUE`, the default there), as one self-contained page with smaller copies of the images (`max_side` 640). In select mode, **Copy R code** gives a line such as `chosen <- read_face_selection("te-selection:...", faces)` to run in a new cell, because a page served from R cannot be reached from a notebook running on another computer

o ADD: manual face annotation. `annotation_task()` turns detected faces into a coding task for one or more coders (each face shown outlined in its picture with a close-up; a first question marks detector mistakes, then emotion and ratings; `annotation_questions()` has ready-made questions and takes your own). `annotate_faces()` starts the coding page from R and saves every answer in the task's folder as it is given, so coders can stop and continue. `read_annotations()`, `annotation_progress()`, `annotation_agreement()` (Krippendorff's alpha, Fleiss' kappa, percent agreement) and `consensus_labels()` turn the answers into agreement figures and a truth for `evaluate_emotions()`. In a Jupyter notebook such as Google Colab, `annotate_faces()` shows the coding page below the cell, and **Copy R code** gives a line, `answers <- read_annotations(task, "te-annotations:...")`, to run in a new cell. The new vignette "Checking faces and coding them by hand" walks through it

o ADD: `annotation_task(items = ...)` makes a coding task of video clips, images or texts instead of single faces, for example the clips a model got wrong. Coders see sampled frames of each clip (`frames`, default 4) with its face boxes, the largest face (the one `video_scores()` scores by default) drawn solid, the clip itself with its sound (`play`), and the item's text. With a reference `label` and a model `prediction` in the items, the page shows them and the model's scores once the coder has answered (`blind = TRUE`), then asks what explains the result: the new ready-made question `annotation_questions("why")` (the model is wrong, the label is debatable, the wrong face was scored, no face is visible, they agree). Questions can wait for that with `reveal = TRUE`. `annotate_faces()` runs these tasks too, in the browser or in a notebook; `read_annotations()` and `consensus_labels()` return each item's `id`, `reference`, `prediction` and scores, ready for `evaluate_emotions()`

o ADD: example files `two-people.jpg` (two people side by side) and `two-people.mp4` (12 s: two people, a black gap, then one of them alone) in `inst/extdata`, for trying the face functions

o ADD: `face_selection` in `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` also takes `"all"` (one row per face), face numbers (`image_scores()`), or a data frame from `detect_faces()` or `select_faces()`, for example the faces of one person in a video

o ADD: same-person recognition uses OpenCV's SFace model (10 MB, Apache 2.0), downloaded on first use or by `setup_modules()`; set `TRANSFOREMOTION_SFACE_MODEL` to use a copy on a computer without internet access. Face embeddings stay in memory and are never returned or saved; `identify = FALSE` turns recognition off

o ADD: `calibrate_scores()` and `posterior_scores()` treat frame scores as noisy evidence instead of taking them at face value. `calibrate_scores()` learns a model's average score for each class (its lean towards some labels) and, from videos with known emotions, its confusions; `posterior_scores()` combines each frame with the frames before it (a hidden Markov model over the emotions; `method = "smooth"` also uses the frames after it) and returns posteriors for every frame and for each clip. On held-out CREMA-D clips with six labels, clip accuracy against the face-only vote rose from 0.55 to 0.59 without labels and to 0.61 with labels for "oai-base-fer", and from 0.28 to 0.37 and 0.40 for "oai-base"; the top label of a frame changes about 0.5 times a second instead of 3 to 4

o CHANGE: faces are found with OpenCV's YuNet detector (a 230 KB model shipped with the package) instead of a Haar cascade. YuNet also finds small, tilted and partly turned faces, so the face that is scored, and therefore the scores, can differ from earlier versions; `face_detector = "haar"` in `image_scores()`, `image_scores_dir()` and `video_scores()` reproduces earlier results

o CHANGE: with YuNet, images are rotated by their EXIF orientation before faces are detected, as phone photos are displayed

o FIX: `video_scores()` scored the wrong frames after a frame that could not be read: it looked frames up by a running count, so it scored a missing (or leftover) frame file and dropped the last frame. Unreadable frames are now left out, and the result has one row per frame that was read

o FIX: `video_scores(uniform = TRUE)` failed with "Video is too short for requested number of frames" whenever `ffreq * nframes` exceeded the video's length, although uniform sampling does not use `ffreq`, and could ask for frames past the end. Frames are now spread evenly over the whole video (or the `start`-`end` cut), and a cut video no longer samples frames past `end`

o FIX: `evaluate_emotions()` reported Scott's pi as Krippendorff's alpha; the expected disagreement now counts pairs of values without replacement, as Krippendorff's alpha does, so alpha is slightly higher for small samples

o FIX: `image_scores_dir(recursive = TRUE)` warns when images in different folders share a file name, since their `image_id` is the same

o FIX: the documentation said that an image without a face returns `NULL`; it returns one row of `NA` scores


